<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nigeria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nigeria is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, created as a British colony in the early twentieth century and independent since 1960. Its territory includes the heartlands of several empires covered on the site, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Nigeria has been central to the debate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> looted by British forces in 1897.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nigeria.jpg" alt="Nigeria" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Harald Søby. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nigeria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nigeria lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the south. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. The Niger and Benue rivers converge in a Y shape and empty into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a>, one of the world's largest river deltas. The highest point is Chappal Waddi, at 2,419 metres.</p><p>The far south has a tropical rainforest climate, with mangrove swamps along the coast. Between the far south and the far north lies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a>, which divides into Guinean forest-savanna mosaic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> savannah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> savannah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nok civilisation thrived between 1500 BCE and 200 CE. It produced life-sized terracotta figures that are among the earliest known sculptures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and it independently invented iron smelting by about 550 BCE and possibly a few centuries earlier. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> records the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> city state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> from around 999 CE, and other Hausa cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a>, Katsina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> have recorded histories dating to the 10th century.</p><p>The Kingdom of Nri of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo people</a> consolidated in the 10th century and lasted until it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the British in 1911, and the oldest bronzes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> made by the lost-wax process come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a>, a city under Nri influence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> had developed one of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> at Ile Ife by the 8th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a>, had a densely populated capital that served as a hub for regional and transatlantic trade in cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and salt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The name Nigeria was coined on 8 January 1897 by the British journalist Flora Shaw, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>. The modern state took its present shape in 1914 with the merging of the Southern Nigeria Protectorate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Northern Nigeria Protectorate</a>. Independence as a federation came on 1 October 1960, followed by a civil war from 1967 to 1970, a series of military governments, and civilian rule from 1999.</p><p>Nigeria is a federal republic of 36 states and the Federal Capital Territory, with a bicameral National Assembly. More than 250 ethnic groups live there, speaking about 500 languages, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> together make up over 60% of the population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nigeria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Benin Empire grew out of a gradual process of political centralisation in the forest lowlands of what is now southern <b>Nigeria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire left an extensive and durable legal inheritance: English common law, procedural traditions and constitutional frameworks were transplanted into dozens of successor states, and the Westminster parliamentary model was adopted, with varying success, across the former empire from India to Jamaica to <b>Nigeria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hausa kingdoms grew out of farming communities on the savanna of what is now northern <b>Nigeria</b> and southern Niger.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and the Oyo people lost the northern portion of their region, refugees poured into Ibadan, and when the Sokoto Caliphate attempted to expand further into the south of what is now <b>Nigeria</b> it was decisively defeated by the armies of Ibadan in 1840, which eventually halted its progress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ibadan">Ibadan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lunsi tell of Tohazhie, the Red Hunter, who left Tunga east of Lake Chad with a small band of horsemen for Zamfara in present-day <b>Nigeria</b> and then Mali, where he married Pag Wabiga, daughter of the king, and fathered Kpoginumbo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ilé-Ifẹ̀ is an ancient Yoruba city in south-western <b>Nigeria</b>, founded at some time between 1000 and 500 BCE; by 900 CE it had become an important West African emporium producing sophisticated art.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
